## Series context & the key point _(why this concept, not a generic one)_

Part 2.0 is the **second beat of the 4-part Fit arc** (1: the goal → **2: structure of a day** → 3: the program → 4: pharmacology). Part 1 set the targets (recomp/cut/bulk + maintenance calories). Part 2's job is to turn those targets into a **daily operating procedure** — and the author is explicit that once Part 1 is done, the day collapses to **two variables: Demand (workout intensity) and Supply (food intake).**

But the article's real intellectual core — the thing every habit in it is secretly serving — is **Fuel Partitioning.** Quote: _"When you eat, your body has to decide: does this energy go to muscle, or does it go to fat? This is governed by insulin sensitivity."_ High sensitivity → insulin opens the **GLUT4** transporters and glucose flows into muscle. Resistance → the same calories go to fat. Every single block of the day (fasting, carb timing, the workout, the post-meal walk, the Pomodoro stack) exists to **keep that switch flipped toward muscle.** The day is not a schedule; it's a control sequence for a fuel router.

**The single key point to make a reader feel:** _The same calorie can build muscle or build fat — the day decides which. Time your carbs and your movement and the switch stays on "muscle"; sit still after carbs and it flips to "fat."_ A quiet second lesson the visual can teach for free: **the day is engineered to be mostly self-correcting — there is exactly one place it goes wrong (carbs + sitting still), and one habit that fixes it almost everywhere (move after you eat).**

This is the concept other chats miss: they'd draw a sunrise-to-moonrise daily schedule with food icons and a checklist. The _real_ payload of this specific article is the **partitioning state running underneath the clock** — why each block exists, not just when it happens.

